WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.859
Film Courage: Is it true that beginning writers need
the most help with structure?  Writer/Instructor and Script Consultant Shannan E. Johnson (The Professional Pen): No.

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:08.760
I think all writers need help with
structure. I think there are writers

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.340
right now who are employed and writers
rooms who have sold features before

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:20.039
and they need help with structure I
definitely do and the way that you know

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:24.960
that that's true is because there are
writers who have jobs a lot of the

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.410
times when someone sells a feature just
because they sold it doesn't mean that's

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.980
the way it's gonna make it onto the
screen right so they may buy it because

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.739
they like the idea they may buy it
because they like the characters they

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:37.260
may buy it because they like the world
they may buy it because they like you

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.850
you never know things happen but before
that thing goes to the next round

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.700
they're gonna hire someone else to come
in and tighten it up to come in and

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.129
restructure to come in and give it what
it needs and this is something that I do

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:54.180
as well I work with a lot of writers who
have something great on the page but

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.410
structurally speaking it's not hitting
the beats that it needs to hit like when

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:04.890
you if your script gets on someone's
desk finally makes it in they're gonna

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.630
have their intern or their assistant
read it first somebody who may or may

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.049
not have ever written a thing in their
lives somebody who may or may not have a

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:17.909
degree someone who may have just gotten
their day one and they're gonna be the

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:21.479
people who read your script they're
gonna have to read the entire thing

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:24.270
because that's their job and then
they're gonna write coverage on it which

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.439
means they're gonna say this is good
this is bad this is ugly these are what

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:32.220
the characters are executive you should
or you should not read it that's

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.180
basically what the coverage is going to
do the executive will then say okay

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.700
they're gonna take it and because they
have eight million scripts on their desk

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.259
they're gonna give it about 10 to 15
pages to hit particular beats before

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:51.390
they decide to continue to read there's
kind of like a industry standard of you

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.649
got to give it at least the 10 first 10
or 15 pages some people including myself

00:01:55.649 --> 00:02:00.210
can give it three pages and know this
isn't gonna it's not gonna do what it's

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.810
supposed to do but because I'm a script
consultant I almost have to read you

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.469
know everything so I usually can't just
give it a few pages I have to read

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:10.830
everything so that I can give detailed
notes about what's going on but as far

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.260
as an executive goes they know what
they're looking

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.810
first of all wherever it is they're
working has a brand so if they're at Fox

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:24.329
they're not gonna buy what CBS would buy
they're not gonna buy what NBC would buy

00:02:24.329 --> 00:02:28.709
they're not gonna buy what own would buy
they each have their own brand their own

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:32.670
audiences and things that they're
looking for so outside of looking for

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.030
those story beasts are also looking for
but would we put this on our channel

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.440
because regardless to how great it is if
it's not something that would be on

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.010
their channel they're not going to
continue to read it it's a waste of

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.299
their time to continue to read it so
with that being said structure matters

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:52.859
as far as Hollywood goes if you're an
independent writer an independent

00:02:52.859 --> 00:02:55.260
producer independent director you can
write what you want to write and make

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.620
what you want to make because it's your
money but as far as Hollywood goes there

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.200
are particular things that they're
looking for if we haven't hit that

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.989
inciting incident around page 15 they're
not gonna keep reading when I used to

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:10.889
teach at Florida State
I taught intro to film and the whole

00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:14.639
class was we would watch films and then
they would have to analyze them in the

00:03:14.639 --> 00:03:19.590
written form and I would always say if I
get your essay and I haven't hit the

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.069
thesis statement I would even give them
the whole page even though we all know

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:26.250
these are statement should be in the
first paragraph but if I get to the

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.970
bottom of page one and I haven't gotten
to the thesis statement I'm not

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:35.209
continuing to read because you're making
me guess what your paper is about I

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:39.629
shouldn't have to guess what it's about
you should tell me this is what this

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:44.040
thing is about or this is what I'm going
to try to prove to you or whatever

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.599
that's what the inciting incident is for
the screenplay for you to give me the

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:53.970
information of this is what this thing
is about this is what my character is

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:58.260
going to be doing for all of act 2 or
this is what goal my character is trying

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.220
to reach in the resolution if I can't
know that by page 15 I'm not gonna keep

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.319
looking for it you know and so that's
why structure matters and there are some

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:11.639
people who don't who write who naturally
write well and don't know that they're

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:14.819
writing in that structure and then there
are some people who have great ideas and

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:18.239
great characters and great worlds and
the reason that the things aren't

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:21.570
selling or aren't doing what they need
to do is because they don't have any

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:26.190
structure so I think everybody can learn
from structure every single person some

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.680
people the people who
mastered it are those people who are

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.790
consistently making money as writers
because if you understand structure you

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:37.650
can write anybody story not just your
own and a lot of us think that being a

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.370
writer is all about writing our own
ideas and there are some people who get

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.200
to that level that their writing their
own ideas but most professional writers

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.800
people who are consistently getting paid
are people who are being hired by

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.690
producers to write for them or hire to
do rewrites on somebody else's stuff or

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.230
someone who can jump from writers room
to writers room to writers room and

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:02.520
continue to go up in in rank in those
writers rooms because they can hear

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.880
someone else's story and because they
know structure they can make it happen

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.810
so structure is just going to be helpful
all the way around is there one

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:14.160
structure upon which almost every story
will fit into there are several

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.460
different types of structures and it
depends on what kind of story you're

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.740
telling
so we have three act structure and

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.010
that's the one that almost everybody
knows and in my opinion three act

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.490
structure is the best to know because
even if you're writing in five acts all

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.900
it means is that you're stretching out
your 3x right or if you're writing in 2x

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.800
you're convincing your 2x or for those
those people who are into theater if

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.630
you're writing a one-act play your three
acts is happening in one act so

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.919
understanding three act structure which
basically means you pick you spend one

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:49.620
act on setting up you spend one act on
actually getting to the to the goal and

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.520
you spend another act on saying hey I
got the goal now what and that's

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.560
basically what the struck what structure
is so a lot of people will write pages

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:06.300
and pages and pages of activity but
activity is not action right action is

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.160
intentional right people are taking
intentional steps towards a goal

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.240
activity is just anything I went to a
party I sat in my house I watch TV I did

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.190
X Y &amp; Z but if that isn't attached to
you actually trying to reach a goal than

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.820
what am i watching I'm watching the 12
hours of a reality TV show when someone

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:28.710
just sat there all day and filmed let's
say they were filming us right now what

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:32.729
would they be seeing just you sitting
here right but what does that mean

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:37.979
however if they were filming about how
what it takes for you to make your show

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:41.419
happen
now this becomes an intentional part of

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:46.639
the story but if the story is about you
going to the grocery store today then

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:52.820
why would we need to be showing this
right so yes there is one particular

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:56.780
structure that if everybody learns it's
gonna be helpful to you but most

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:01.009
features are written in three acts
someone our dramas urban in three acts

00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:07.490
some are written in five and in comedies
half-hour comedies those can be written

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.900
in 2x or also three acts depending upon
what kind of comedies they are

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:16.880
single-camera comedies are usually done
in three acts multi-camera comedies

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.020
might be done in two so what I say to
people is going back to that homework

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:25.280
going back to honing your craft is read
scripts that are close to the script

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.030
that you're writing so that you can see
what kind of structure and what kind of

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:34.880
format they use because even the format
can change from features to TV from one

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:39.169
hour to half-hour so in order to know
that and those are those things where

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:42.530
it's like well I'm gonna walk in on my
date on my first day and be showrunner

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.500
it's like yes but you don't know that
there's a different format between

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.180
half-hour multi-camera comedy and a
single-camera comedy that's information

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:53.210
that you should probably know if you're
gonna run the show right and the

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:57.020
information is out there because now we
live in 2020 and there's YouTube there's

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.070
Instagram and there are people who are
giving the information away for free so

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:05.870
when people don't take the opportunity
to use utilize those resources then

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:09.940
that's when I go back to you got to
respect the craft

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.830
you

